or low back pain  and migraine patients  what problem did you have with that oh  that was trouble you you're talking  about a class two narcotic and you're  giving it to a migraine patient so it  puts patients at risk boys was fired  when the company learned he was working  with the FDA and then his fears about  promoting act eek for off-label use were  realized Robin Geist ouack a migraine  sufferer who took act eek to dull her  headaches became tragically addicted boy  I wish Robin had never taken that acting  I think she would still be alive  Peter Chatfield is boys's whistleblower  attorney in light of the opioid crisis  that we all know about would it be fair  to say that Bruce was the canary in the  coal mine I think with hindsight about  what has happened it absolutely was we  had never seen anything like that and  you can see what happened it took off  from there and Alex Ferrer joins us now  Alex good morning an opioid lollipop is  a serious thing so what are the FDA  learn putting a wire on a salesperson  that used to work with this product well  one of the things they learned was how  open and blatant this illegal activity  was it's illegal to market drugs  off-label for pharmaceutical company and  here was the director of marketing  standing on the stage at the national  sales meeting telling the entire sales  force are bragging about the fact that  they're selling all these different  drugs off-label which is illegal and one  of them act eek the one we're talking  about you know you had you had people  who were found basically dead with a  lollipop in their mouth because they  should not have been taking act ich or  incredibly powerful fentanyl is a  hundred times more powerful than  morphine right Alex thank you very much  scary stuff you can watch whistleblower  opioid lollipops the case against  Cephalon tonight it's part of a double  feature season finale that begins at  8:00 7:00 central right here on CBS
 

